Post Nut Calamity - Edi device integration

  mod version    PncEdi @VERSION@
  built for      Post Nut Calamity @GAME@

Plays a funscript on your stroker that matches whatever is happening on screen: grab scenes,
peepholes, the dioramas in the world, and every entry in the gallery. @ROWS@ scenes, each one
timed against the game's own animation rather than scripted to video, in two device variants.

Works on the Windows build (directly or under Proton) and on the native Linux build, from these
same files.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The mod does not talk to your device. It tells Edi which scene just started, and Edi plays the
matching funscript. So Edi has to be running whenever you play.

Edi @EDITAG@ is included in this archive - you do not need to download anything else.

The bundled Edi is Edi v1.0.4 with one fix applied that has not been released upstream yet.
Without it, the device stalls and drifts out of time whenever a looping scene is resumed - which
includes every pause and unpause - and stays that way until the next scene change. The fix is
NoGRo/Edi pull request #15 (https://github.com/NoGRo/Edi/pull/15), reported as issue #14; nothing
else about Edi is changed. Once it is in a released Edi, this archive will simply ship that
instead.

If you would rather run stock Edi, or already have Edi installed, see "Already have Edi
installed" below - the mod works with any Edi, it just plays worse on an unpatched one.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Extract this archive into your Post Nut Calamity folder - the one holding the game executable
   and the "Post Nut Calamity_Data" folder. Keep the layout; say yes to merging folders.

2. Run Edi\Edi.exe. It opens a window and a local web UI; leave it running while you play.

   Everything it needs is already beside it: Edi\EdiConfig.json is set up, and the funscripts
   are in Edi\Gallery, which is exactly where that config points. Nothing to configure.

   Your device appears in the config the first time Edi sees it. That is the one thing you do
   have to touch - see step 4.

   Already have Edi installed elsewhere and would rather keep it? Run that one instead, and copy
   this archive's Edi\Gallery folder next to its EdiConfig.json.

3. Start the game:

   Windows, and the Windows build under Proton
       Just launch the game as usual. Nothing else to do - winhttp.dll loads the mod.

   Native Linux build
       ./start-pnc-linux.sh

       (Or BepInEx's own script if you prefer: ./run_bepinex.sh ./<the game>.x86_64)

       Note that Edi itself is a Windows program. Run it under Wine/Proton, or on another
       machine and point [EDI] Url in the config at it - the mod only needs to reach it over
       HTTP.

4. In Edi, set your device's "Variant" to the folder that matches your hardware - "handy2pro",
   "handy2" or "handy1". A device left on "None" is muted, which looks exactly like the mod not
   working. See "DEVICE VARIANTS" below.

   UPGRADING FROM 2.5.2 OR EARLIER: the variant folders are now named after the devices they are
   for. What used to be called "detailed" is now "handy2pro". If your EdiConfig.json still says
   "Variant": "detailed", change it, or Edi will look for a folder that is no longer there.

You should now see a BepInEx console line on startup, and BepInEx/LogOutput.log should fill with
[EDI] lines as scenes play.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEVICE VARIANTS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three sets of the same scripts are included. Edi picks one by the "Variant" set on the device in
EdiConfig.json, and the variant name is the folder name:

  handy2pro   The masters, and the default. Written for a Handy 2 Pro with the slider overclock
              turned up (1200 mm/s over a 125 mm stroke = 960 units/s, off the device's own
              overclocking menu). Fast accents are kept deliberately: a snap the motor renders as
              "as fast as you can" is part of the script.

  handy2      Held to 600 units/s sustained, 700 peak - the soft and hard caps the eroscripts
              multi-axis scripting guide gives for a Handy 2. For a Handy 2, an OSR/SR6, or a
              2 Pro that is overclocked part-way or not at all and where the loud scenes feel like
              too much. Sixteen of the 102 scripts differ from the masters; the rest are
              identical, because they were already inside those caps.

              A 2 Pro with no overclock at all is 450 mm/s = 360 units/s, which is what handy1 is
              limited to - so that is the better folder for one, despite the name.

  handy1      Speed-limited for a Handy 1 (110 mm at 400 mm/s = 364 units/s). Slow strokes keep
              their full range; only transitions the device cannot physically reach are shortened.

In every variant the TIMING is identical - it is the sync to the animation and never moves. Only
strokes the device could not have completed are shortened, so a slower variant loses reach on the
loud scenes and changes nothing anywhere else.

Set it like this in EdiConfig.json:

    "The Handy [xxxxxxxx]": { "Variant": "handy2pro", "Channel": null, "Min": 0, "Max": 100 }


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIGURATION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Press F11 in game, or use the MOD MANAGER button on the main menu. It lists every loaded BepInEx
plugin, searches, and saves as you edit. Most settings take effect immediately; the ones that do
not say so in their own description.

The same settings live in BepInEx/config/com.edi.pnc.cfg, one comment per setting saying what it
does and what its default is. BepInEx rewrites that file on each launch, which reorders entries
but keeps your values.

A few worth knowing about:

  [Gameplay] Profile               One coherent set of rules instead of the individual switches
                                   having to agree. See GAMEPLAY PROFILES below.
  [Gameplay] EnableHeatLocks       Horny locks replace vanilla's survivability: your heat cannot
                                   drop below a floor until a peephole or diorama releases it.
  [Gameplay] GodMode               Off by default here, because heat locks replace it. Turn it on
                                   if you want to browse rather than play.
  [Gameplay] EndGrabDelaySeconds   How long a scene runs before you can leave it (Q).
  [EDI]      Url                   Where Edi is listening. Default http://127.0.0.1:5000.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GAMEPLAY PROFILES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[Gameplay] Profile picks one of four, and can be switched mid-run from the mod manager:

  Vanilla              The game's own combat, health, heat and spawning, untouched. Scene
                       detection and device playback stay on - the mod still plays every scene.
  PressureAndRelease   Heat locks, class heat scaling and lock-scaled spawn pressure, together.
                       Each qualifying scene adds a lock; your heat cannot fall below what those
                       locks hold until a peephole or diorama releases them, and waves grow from
                       vanilla size at no locks to 2.5x at full lock. The intended way to play.
  GodMode              Damage and death blocked, locks off. For exploring and filling the gallery.
  Custom               The default, and what an existing config keeps doing: the individual
                       Gameplay settings are read exactly as they were before profiles existed.

Switching clears transient locks and reapplies the vanilla player stats it observed at startup,
so a switch out of God Mode cannot leave you with its health.

[EDI] HandyIntensitySource decides which figure drives the idle filler scripts (filler_cum_25 /
_50 / _75): Automatic uses heat under Vanilla and God Mode and the stronger of heat and lock
pressure under Pressure and Release; Heat and ReleasePressure force one or the other; Highest
always takes whichever is stronger.

[EDI] ChaserAura, on by default, builds the idle filler as a chaser boss closes in: while the
dragon's or the wendigo's approach loop is audible, the device's stroke range is scaled by how
close it is - shallow at the edge of the band, full at the range where it grabs. It never changes
which script is playing and never makes anything louder than the script asks for, and it hands the
range straight back when a real scene starts. ChaserAuraRange is how wide the band is in metres,
ChaserAuraIntensityFar how shallow its far end, and ChaserAuraRamp how fast the change is allowed
to move - lower that if it reads as abrupt, or set ChaserAura = false to turn it off.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CUSTOM ENEMIES AND WALL TRAPS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The mod can load enemy packages that are pure data - a manifest, PNG sprite sheets and funscripts
in a folder. No Unity Editor needed for the 2D kind. They get their own gallery tab, their own
Edi rows, and a switch each in the mod manager.

Drop a package into BepInEx/custom-enemies/ and restart. The format, both kinds, is documented in:

  BepInEx/custom-enemies/README.md               which kind is which
  BepInEx/custom-enemies/CUSTOM-ENEMIES.md       roaming enemies, artwork, the witch boss
  BepInEx/custom-enemies/WALL-PICTURE-TRAPS.md   pictures that pull you off the wall

No packages ship in this archive - the format does.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IF IT DOES NOT WORK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing happens at all
    Check BepInEx/LogOutput.log exists. If it does not, BepInEx never loaded: on Windows confirm
    winhttp.dll sits beside the game executable; on Linux launch through start-pnc-linux.sh
    rather than the executable directly.

The log fills with [EDI] lines but the device is silent
    Edi is not running, is on a different port, or the device's Variant is "None" (which mutes
    it) or "detailed" (the pre-2.6.0 name for what is now "handy2pro"). Set it to a folder that
    exists under Edi/Gallery/.

One scene does nothing
    grep the log for ALIAS-GAP:

        grep "\[ALIAS-GAP\]" BepInEx/LogOutput.log

    That names any animation the mod saw and had no script for. Please report it with the line.

The filler goes shallow when a boss is nearby
    That is the chaser aura, and it is deliberate - see ChaserAura under GAMEPLAY PROFILES above.
    [EDI] ChaserAura = false turns it off; ChaserAuraIntensityFar raises its quiet end.

A scene is too strong
    Two knobs under [EDI], both live-editable from F11, and neither one touches the funscripts:

        MasterIntensity      a percentage applied to everything, e.g. 70
        RowIntensityScale    per row, e.g. imp_3=60;imp_3_Gallery=60

    Both work through Edi's intensity endpoint, which moves the top of the device's stroke range
    while the script keeps playing - so a scene gets shallower, not slower, and nothing is
    re-dispatched or retimed. Row names are the ones in Edi/Gallery/Definitions.csv.

    If the problem is that fast sections feel mushy or rattly rather than too deep, that is speed
    and not strength: switch to the "handy2" or "handy1" variant instead. See DEVICE VARIANTS.

To remove the mod
    Delete winhttp.dll, doorstop_config.ini, .doorstop_version, libdoorstop.so, run_bepinex.sh,
    start-pnc-linux.sh, the BepInEx folder and the Edi folder. The game is untouched otherwise -
    nothing in this archive overwrites a game file.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS IN HERE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  BepInEx/plugins/PncEdi.dll        the mod
  BepInEx/plugins/PncModManager.dll the in-game settings window (F11)
  BepInEx/plugins/PncCustomEnemies.dll
                                    the custom-enemy framework - loads the packages below
  BepInEx/config/com.edi.pnc.cfg    its settings
  BepInEx/custom-enemies/           the custom-enemy format: documentation and a template
  BepInEx/core/                     BepInEx @BEPINEX@ (unmodified)
  winhttp.dll, doorstop_config.ini  Windows loader (Unity Doorstop @DOORSTOP@, unmodified)
  libdoorstop.so, run_bepinex.sh    Linux loader (same, unmodified)
  start-pnc-linux.sh                launcher that points run_bepinex.sh at the game
  Edi/Edi.exe                       Edi @EDITAG@ - see HOW IT WORKS above
  Edi/EdiConfig.json                Edi's settings, pointed at the gallery beside it
  Edi/Gallery/                      the funscripts, @SCRIPTS@ per variant
  PncEdi-CHANGELOG.txt              what changed, in detail
  PncEdi-CREDITS.txt                who made what this is built on

No game files are included in this archive.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CREDITS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Post Nut Calamity is by ShoeStrangNBoydy. Nothing of the game is in this archive.

Edi is by NoGRo: https://github.com/NoGRo/Edi. Which build is bundled, and whether anything is
changed in it, is stated under HOW IT WORKS above.

This mod continues the PncEdi mod from its Eroscripts thread, which by then was every release on
that thread pasted over each other in order: everydayhandyuser's original integration, edale's
gallery-unlock keys, overkeks' 0.2.1 edit, and Dupli9d's patch and horny-system build. The mod
manager, the gameplay profiles and the custom-enemy framework came from Dupli9d's
PncEdi-portable-patch.

The gallery started as funscripts written by other people, and some rows still descend from them:
everydayhandyuser's original gallery scripts and the OpenFunscripter-authored zombie and plantasha
scripts that shipped with them, Dupli9d's new.funscript, mr_spunky's imp scripts and play-through
captures, and AniFS's ambient and peek scripts.

The thread, and where releases of this mod are posted:
https://discuss.eroscripts.com/t/game-integration-post-nut-calamity-0-1-0-edi/315695

PncEdi-CREDITS.txt has the full version.

